WEBVTT

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.080
Tokens per second approximates generation speed after processing.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.290
Time to first token includes request setup and prompt evaluation before visible output.

00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:11.645
Prompt-processing speed becomes especially important for long documents, code,

00:00:11.645 --> 00:00:13.310
and retrieved context.

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:15.240
Latency describes how long one request waits,

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:18.195
while throughput describes total work completed across time.

00:00:18.195 --> 00:00:21.600
A setting that improves batch throughput may increase interactive latency.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.380
Multi-user services require queue and concurrency measurements.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:27.833
GPU offload can accelerate supported layers or operations.

00:00:27.833 --> 00:00:31.250
CPU fallback allows models to run when accelerator memory is limited,

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:32.480
but it may reduce speed.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:36.560
The best offload level depends on memory, bandwidth, runtime, and workload.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:40.305
Thermal throttling reduces sustained clocks when heat or power limits are reached.

00:00:40.305 --> 00:00:42.870
Monitor performance over a long enough test to expose it.

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:46.760
A short cold-start result can misrepresent a laptop or compact system.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:49.125
Benchmark end-to-end tasks, not only generation.

00:00:49.125 --> 00:00:53.320
Include model load, prompt processing, first token, output, structured parsing,

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.791
tool calls, and failure recovery where relevant.

00:00:55.791 --> 00:00:58.135
Record memory, power, temperature, and errors.

00:00:58.135 --> 00:01:00.220
Set practical expectations.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:03.035
Interactive writing may tolerate one speed, batch summarization another,

00:01:03.035 --> 00:01:06.800
and agent automation may care more about task success than raw tokens.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:11.200
The right model delivers acceptable work within the user's patience and system capacity.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:12.850
Measure variability, not only averages.

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:17.040
A system with acceptable average latency may still produce long pauses during large

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:17.680
prompts,

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:21.340
model switching, concurrent requests, cache growth, thermal throttling, or memory cleanup.
